GitOps Architecture Deep Dive

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring the GitOps Architecture and how different components work together to automate deployments. 🚀
Understanding this architecture helps us see how Git, automation tools, and Kubernetes collaborate to maintain reliable infrastructure.

🔹 Git Repository
 The Git repository acts as the single source of truth.
 It stores all configuration files such as:
Kubernetes YAML manifests
Infrastructure configuration files
Application deployment definitions
Any change in the system must first go through Git commits and pull requests.

🔹 Operator / Controller
 A GitOps operator continuously monitors the Git repository and the cluster.
 Its responsibilities include:
Detecting changes in Git
Comparing desired state with actual state
Applying updates automatically to the cluster
This ensures deployments remain consistent and automated.

🔹 Kubernetes Cluster
 The Kubernetes cluster is where the actual applications and services run.
 The GitOps controller ensures the cluster state always matches what is defined in Git.
If any configuration drift occurs, the system automatically reconciles the difference.

🔹 CI Tools Integration
 CI tools play an important role in the pipeline by:
Building application code
Running automated tests
Updating configuration repositories with new versions
After CI completes, the GitOps controller detects the change and deploys it to the cluster automatically.

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps architecture creates a fully automated deployment workflow where Git controls infrastructure, applications, and system state.
